723:
(a) Generally.
(1) All persons before beginning the business of operating, maintaining or promoting an indoor mall type flea market shall first file an application with the city clerk for a license to conduct such business. Such application shall be made on a form supplied by the city clerk and shall contain such information necessary to properly regulate the business;
(2) No outdoor flea markets will be allowed in the city limits of Rossville, Georgia;
(3) The chief of police or his designee shall cause an investigation to be made into the information contained in the application of the promoter including the background, character, and reputation of said promoter. Upon completion of the investigation the chief of police or his designee shall report the results of said investigation to the city clerk, whose duty it shall be to make a decision on the issuance of the license;
(4) The city clerk shall have the duty and authority to revoke the license of any flea market when in the discretion of the mayor and council such revocation is necessary to prevent any violation of this article or any other ordinance of the city or any laws of the state or of the United States. The selling of firearms and fireworks is prohibited and will result in the revoking of the license; and
(5) The city will require the owner/promoter of the indoor flea market to keep written records, giving names and descriptions of each vendor, vendor's place of residence and street address. Such records must be made available to the city upon request.
(b) License fees.
(1) The owner/promoter of an indoor flea market will be charged a flat fee of $10.00 per month, per booth rented and shall remit to the city the total amount due on the last day of each month;
(2) The city clerk will issue a license to the owner/promoter of said indoor flea market on a monthly basis when fees are paid; and
(3) It will be the responsibility of the owner/promoter of the indoor flea market to maintain and provide records to the city clerk when requested on each vendor engaged in business at the flea market.
(Ord. No. 391, 6-9-03; Ord. No. 392, 7-14-03)
Editor's note— The provisions of Ord. Nos. 391, adopted June 9, 2003, and 392, adopted July 14, 2003, have replaced the provisions of former § 58-71, which pertained to the prohibition of flea markets, and derived from § 13-89 of the 1976 Code.

728:
Regulations of the location, size, placement and certain features of signs is necessary, to enable the public to locate goods, services, and facilities in the city without difficulty and confusion, to improve the general attractiveness of the community, to take advantage of the beauty of the community's natural environment, and to protect property values therein. Accordingly, it is the intention of the city to establish regulations governing the display of signs which will:
